---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/release.css';
import StageTabs from '../../components/StageTabs.astro';
import ReleaseLog from '../../components/ReleaseLog.astro';

const title = 'Familiar | A companion for your newsletter';
const description =
  'Familiar sits beside you while you work and then helps you write about it: questions that pull out what only you know, notes from your coding sessions drafted into your voice, and an editor\'s report to collaborate on.';

const stages = [
  {
    n: '01',
    name: 'Interview',
    cmd: 'familiar interview "why our churn model kept lying"',
    what: 'One question at a time, starting with what happened to you. Every few answers it tries the thesis back at you. You end with a thesis in one sentence, the stakes, an evidence list, and the lines worth building on.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    name: 'Outline',
    cmd: 'familiar outline',
    what: 'Three shapes that are genuinely different: argument first, scene first, or the reader\'s problem first. Each with where your best line lands and one way it could fail. You pick, merge or veto. It will not draft until you have.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    name: 'Draft',
    cmd: 'familiar draft',
    what: 'About eighty percent of a draft you will make excellent, in your voice. Where it would have invented a number or a quote, it leaves a bracket and says what it needed.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    name: 'Dev edit',
    cmd: 'familiar dev-edit',
    what: 'The demanding, loyal editor. Where the spark is, whether the argument still holds, what has to move, each with an exact rewrite. A report, which you work through yourself. Your draft is untouched.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    name: 'Line edit',
    cmd: 'familiar line-edit',
    what: 'The boring pass, run last. The patterns that give a model away, your house spelling and dash rule, reading ease. When it cannot tell a tell from your dry wit, it says so.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    name: 'Social',
    cmd: 'familiar social next',
    what: 'A week of posts on your own cadence. One pool of candidates, you pick per channel, exact send times. Nothing is scheduled until you confirm; with nothing connected you get a paste-ready list.',
  },
  {
    n: '07',
    name: 'Learn',
    cmd: 'familiar learn diff pieces/2026-09-03-churn',
    what: 'Hand it the issue you sent next to the draft it gave you. The edits you made twice become rules, with the sentence each came from. Or point it at your back catalogue and let it draft your voice files from evidence.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="rel">
    <section class="rel-hero wrap">
      <div class="rel-hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Familiar</p>
        <h1>A companion for your <em>newsletter</em>.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Familiar sits beside you while you work and then helps you write
          about the work you do. It asks the questions that pull out what only
          you know, drafts additional notes from your coding sessions into your
          voice, and hands back an editor's report for you to collaborate on so
          that you can show your work and talk about the products you're
          making.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row rel-cta">
          <a class="btn-primary" href="https://github.com/intentionaut/familiar?utm_source=intentionaut.com&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=open-source" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get it on GitHub</a>
          <span class="pill"><span class="pill-prompt">$</span> npx skills add intentionaut/familiar</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="term" role="img" aria-label="A Familiar interview session in a terminal">
        <div class="term-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>familiar · interview</span></div>
        <pre class="term-body"><span class="t-p">$</span> <span class="t-c">familiar interview</span> "the churn model that kept lying"

<span class="t-f">Familiar</span>  Start with what happened. When did you first
          notice it was wrong, and what were you looking at?

<span class="t-y">You</span>       A Tuesday. The model said 4% monthly churn and
          finance said 11%. Same customers.

<span class="t-f">Familiar</span>  Same customers, two numbers. What was each of
          them counting as a customer?

<span class="t-y">You</span>       Ours counted logins. Theirs counted invoices.

<span class="t-f">Familiar</span>  So the piece is arguing that churn is a
          definition problem before it is a data problem?

<span class="t-y">You</span>       Yes. That's it.

<span class="t-d">  → notes.md  thesis · stakes · 3 evidence items (1 needs source)</span><span class="t-cursor"></span></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap rel-story">
      <p class="eyebrow">Where it came from</p>
      <h2>I built this because I kept losing the story of my own work.</h2>
      <div class="rel-story-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I write a fortnightly letter about design, product, data and AI,
            and most of it comes from things I have built. That should make it
            easy to write. It does not, because the good material is the part
            you take for granted. When I asked a model to help, it made things
            worse: it filled in the parts I forgot to say with the most
            plausible thing it could think of, and I caught one inventing a
            statistic for a piece with my name on it. So I built a newsroom for
            myself the way good editors work. Interview first. Propose shapes
            and let the writer choose. Hand back notes, never a rewrite. Then I
            took my own voice out of it and gave it a name: a familiar, in the
            old stories, sits beside the work and notices things.
          </p>
          <p>
            The part I did not plan is the part I value most. My pace of
            thought is not steady. Some days the idea arrives whole and the
            interview is three answers long; some days I circle a paragraph for
            a week and come back with a different opening. Because every stage
            is a file that waits, the work is always exactly where I left it,
            and Familiar picks up at whatever speed I am moving. If your
            attention works in bursts and loops too, the stage gates will feel
            like they were built for you.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="rel-aside">
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Shape</dt><dd>Seven stages, each a plain markdown prompt</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Runs in</dt><dd>Claude Code, opencode, Dex, or a claude.ai Project</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Your voice</dt><dd>Five files you fill in, or let it draft from your back catalogue</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Licence</dt><dd>MIT, free, built in public</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Made by</dt><dd><a href="/">Saielle DaSilva</a>, Intentionaut</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap rel-stages">
      <p class="eyebrow">How a piece moves</p>
      <h2>Seven stages, and it stops after every one of them.</h2>
      <div class="prose rel-stages-intro">
        <p>
          A model left to run a whole pipeline produces something smooth and
          slightly wrong, because each step inherits the last one's small
          errors and nobody corrects them. The gate after each stage puts you
          in between. It also lets you move in both directions: go back to the
          interview and the notes grow, or rework one section and the rest is
          left alone.
        </p>
      </div>
      <StageTabs id="familiar" stages={stages} />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap rel-refuse">
      <p class="eyebrow">What it will not do</p>
      <h2>The restraint is most of the design.</h2>
      <div class="rel-story-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Rewrite tools fail in two ways people keep reporting: they flag the
            things you did on purpose as mistakes, and they drop claims while
            they think they are only changing shape. So Familiar never touches
            your file. Every edit is a proposal you can see, and the cost of a
            wrong one is a glance. Its list of AI tells is checked weekly
            against <a href="https://github.com/blader/humanizer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">humanizer</a>,
            and additions land one at a time with a real example, because a
            bloated list means false positives for every writer using it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="rel-aside">
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Never</dt><dd>Rewrites your file in place</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Never</dt><dd>Invents a number, a quote or a source</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Never</dt><dd>Guesses a voice it has not been shown</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Never</dt><dd>Advances a stage without you</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap rel-voice">
      <div class="voice-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Your voice, your language</p>
          <h2>It learns from what you have published.</h2>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              Ask a writer how they write and you get the writer they would
              like to be. So Familiar reads your past issues instead and drafts
              your voice files from them, with a count next to every claim.
              After each issue, the edits you made twice become rules, and a
              claim you cut is the one it pays most attention to, because it
              usually means the draft overstated.
            </p>
            <p>
              The rules that are really about English (dashes, spelling,
              heading case) are marked and skipped for other languages, and a
              per-language file carries that language's own tells. There are
              none yet, because I can only write one of them well. If you write
              in something other than English, that pull request is the most
              useful one you could open.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="voice-files">
          <div class="file"><span>knowledge/</span>positioning.md<small>what it is, who reads it, house rules</small></div>
          <div class="file"><span>knowledge/</span>voice-guide.md<small>how you write, with the moves that make it yours</small></div>
          <div class="file"><span>knowledge/</span>style-rules.md<small>the tell list the line edit runs</small></div>
          <div class="file"><span>knowledge/examples/</span>canonical.md<small>your own lines at full strength</small></div>
          <div class="file"><span>knowledge/languages/</span>&lt;code&gt;.md<small>rules and tells for a language that is not English</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap rel-log">
      <p class="eyebrow">Activity</p>
      <h2>What has shipped, and when.</h2>
      <ReleaseLog repo="intentionaut/familiar" />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap">
      <div class="rel-close">
        <h2>Free, MIT, and it never sends anything without you.</h2>
        <p>Clone it, fill in two files, and start with the idea that has been rattling around.</p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn-primary" href="https://github.com/intentionaut/familiar?utm_source=intentionaut.com&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=open-source" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit the repo</a>
          <a class="link-arrow" href="/projects/familiar/">The short version</a>
        </div>
        <span class="pill pill-dark"><span class="pill-prompt">$</span> git clone https://github.com/intentionaut/familiar.git</span>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
